@startuml 15-hash-chain
!include style.iuml

skinparam linetype ortho
skinparam objectBackgroundColor #F5F7FA
skinparam objectBorderColor #3C4650

object "Genesis" as g #EDF1F6 {
  hash = "000…000"  (64 zeros)
}

object "AuditEntry  seq = 1" as e1 {
  id = "3f2a…"
  request_id = "a91c…"
  ts = 2026-03-04T11:02:19.184203Z
  kind = "traffic"
  payload = {ip, method, path, verdict, …}
  --
  **prev_hash** = 000…000
  **hash** = 7b41…
}

object "AuditEntry  seq = 2" as e2 {
  id = "8c0d…"
  request_id = "a91c…"
  kind = "admin"
  payload = {actor, action, rule, enabled}
  --
  **prev_hash** = 7b41…
  **hash** = e2f9…
}

object "AuditEntry  seq = 3" as e3 {
  id = "1d77…"
  kind = "traffic"
  payload = {…, rule: "sqli", reason: "…"}
  --
  **prev_hash** = e2f9…
  **hash** = 4a0b…
}

object "Sealing function" as f #FFF8E1 {
  hash = SHA-256(
     len‖prev_hash  ‖
     len‖id         ‖
     len‖request_id ‖
     len‖ts         ‖
     len‖kind       ‖
     len‖canonicalJSON(payload)
  )
  ..
  len = field length in 8 big-endian bytes
}

g -right-> e1 : anchor
e1 -right-> e2 : prev_hash = hash(n−1)
e2 -right-> e3

f .up.> e1
f .up.> e2
f .up.> e3

note bottom of f
  **Why each decision matters.**
  • Canonical JSON (sorted keys, no whitespace, no HTML
    escaping): Go randomizes map iteration, so
    fmt.Sprint would give a different hash on every run
    and verification would report tampering on an
    intact chain.
  • Length prefix: without it, {"ab","c"} and {"a","bc"}
    would produce the same digest, and fields could be
    re-partitioned without breaking the hash.
  • id, request_id and kind are part of the digest: outside
    it they could be rewritten without breaking the chain.
  • ts in RFC3339Nano UTC truncated to microseconds, which
    is timestamptz's resolution — the hashed value is
    exactly the one that comes back on a read.
end note

note top of e2
  Altering this entry changes its hash, so the next
  entry's prev_hash stops matching.
  Deleting it breaks the link between 1 and 3.
  In both cases verification names the exact
  position of the break.
end note

@enduml
